{
  "dom_id": "step-q1278",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "If $A=\\{p,q,r,s,t\\}$, which collection of subsets of $A$ is a partition of $A?$",
  "worked_solution": "A partition of a set $A$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $A$ where every element of $A$ is contained in *exactly* one subset. \n\nOf the given collections, only $\\{\\{p,r,t\\},\\{q,s\\}\\}$ is a partition.\n\nThe rest of the collections are not partitions:\n\n- $\\{\\{p,q\\},\\{q,r\\},\\{s,t\\}\\}$ is not a partition because the element $q$ appears in two subsets.\n\n- $\\{\\{p,t\\},\\{q,r\\},\\{p,s\\}\\}$ is not a partition because the element $p$ appears in two subsets.\n\n- $\\{\\{p,q\\},\\{p,q,r,s\\}\\}$ is not a partition because the element $t\\in A$ does not appear in any subset and the elements $p,$ $q$ appear in multiple subsets.\n\n- $\\{\\{p,q,r\\},\\{t\\}\\}$ is not a partition because the element $s\\in A$ does not appear in any subset.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
